# =============================================================================
# Cambridge-Ellis School - environment contract for the Next.js application
# =============================================================================
#
# This is the committed dotenv TEMPLATE. It is never read by running code: the
# only module that touches `process.env` is `lib/env.ts`, which validates two
# separate schemas (one public, one server-only) and returns `undefined` instead
# of throwing when a value is missing. Every other module reads through it, so a
# server-only key cannot be pulled into a client bundle by accident.
#
# Usage:
#
#     cp .env.example .env.local
#
# Copied verbatim and left untouched, this file describes the KEYLESS state, and
# that state is fully supported: `next build` succeeds, the public site renders
# from the committed fallback JSON in `data/fallback/*.json`, and edit mode
# reports itself unavailable rather than erroring. The CI `build-no-env` job
# proves it by building with no Supabase variables present at all. `.env.local`
# and every other `.env.*` variant are gitignored; only this template is
# committed (the `.gitignore` negation `!.env.example` exists for that reason).
#
# THE THREE STATES. The Supabase keys arrive after the project is complete, so
# the contract has to be legible in all three:
#
#   1. NONE PRESENT  - the shipped default. Content comes from committed
#                      fallback JSON. Sign-in is unavailable. Nothing errors.
#   2. SOME PRESENT  - keys exist but the database is not yet seeded, the assets
#                      are not uploaded, or no admin has been bootstrapped. The
#                      adapter STAYS on fallback: it requires both an explicit
#                      `CONTENT_SOURCE=supabase` and a `site_readiness` row that
#                      the parity tool writes only after every gate passes. A
#                      bare presence check would flip a working site onto an
#                      empty database, so presence alone is deliberately not
#                      sufficient.
#   3. READY         - migrations applied, seed loaded, admins bootstrapped,
#                      assets uploaded, readiness row written. Only then does
#                      `CONTENT_SOURCE=supabase` do anything.
#
# NO USER-SPECIFIED RULES exist for this project (`review_rules` returns "No
# user rules provided"), so no rule mandates or constrains any key below. The
# key set is instead closed by the technical specification, and the root
# `README.md` mirrors it key for key. If the two ever disagree, that is a defect
# in one of them - not licence to add a key here.
#
# Every value below is either EMPTY or a safe default - the four credential and
# URL keys ship empty on purpose, so a verbatim copy lands you in state 1 rather
# than in a half-configured state. Never commit a real URL, key, project
# reference or token to this file: CI runs `gitleaks` over the full history and
# the diff, and a committed service-role key is the single worst outcome
# available to this migration.


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONTENT_SOURCE
#   Required : no - defaults to `fallback`
#   Exposure : server only
#   Values   : fallback | supabase
#   Absent   : the source adapter serves the committed fallback JSON from
#              `data/fallback/*.json`.
#
# Set this to `supabase` ONLY after the ordered cutover in the README runbook
# has completed and the readiness row has been written. Setting it early is the
# one way to point a live site at an empty database.
#
# This is also the rollback switch: returning it to `fallback` and redeploying
# restores the public site to the last exported snapshot in one deploy without
# touching the database. That snapshot is only as fresh as the last
# `export-fallback` run, so the rollback is bounded, not loss-free.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONTENT_SOURCE=fallback


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL
#   Required : only for `CONTENT_SOURCE=supabase`, and for sign-in
#   Exposure : server AND client (inlined into the browser bundle at build time)
#   Absent   : the adapter stays on fallback; sign-in reports edit mode
#              unavailable.
#
# Shape: https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
#
# Because `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` values are inlined at BUILD time, changing this
# requires a redeploy - not just a restart. That is also why CI rebuilds inside
# the e2e job after starting its local stack.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY
#   Required : as NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL above
#   Exposure : server AND client
#   Absent   : the adapter stays on fallback; sign-in reports edit mode
#              unavailable.
#
# This is the publishable key - the successor to the anon key. It is safe in a
# browser bundle: it carries no authority of its own, and every read it can
# perform is bounded by row-level security, which restricts anonymous reads to
# published rows. A draft is not fetched-then-hidden; it is not returned.
#
# The legacy name is ALSO ACCEPTED as a fallback, so an existing deployment does
# not have to be renamed. Uncomment it only if you are supplying that form
# instead of the one above - do not set both:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY
#   Required : only for privileged operations
#   Exposure : SERVER ONLY - must never reach a browser bundle
#   Absent   : `lib/supabase/admin.ts` refuses to construct, and invitations,
#              role changes and the upload finalize path each report themselves
#              unavailable rather than crashing.
#
# This is the service-role credential: it bypasses row-level security entirely.
# The module that reads it is guarded by `import "server-only"`, so importing it
# from a client component is a build error rather than a leak. It has no
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix, and adding one would publish full database authority to
# every visitor.
#
# !! DO NOT SCOPE THIS KEY TO PREVIEW !!
#
# A Vercel *Preview* environment variable is available to any deployment of any
# branch - INCLUDING A BRANCH FROM A FORK. Scoping this key to "Production and
# Preview" would therefore hand full database authority to arbitrary code. The
# mistake is a single checkbox in the Vercel UI, which is why it is called out
# here rather than only in the README.
#
# Two arrangements are acceptable, and the README records which is in force:
#
#   a. Previews point at a SEPARATE Supabase project, seeded from the same
#      migrations, so the key a preview holds has no production authority; or
#   b. Previews receive NO secret key at all and run in fallback, read-only
#      mode.
#
# In both cases the production key is scoped to PRODUCTION ONLY.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL
#   Required : no
#   Exposure : server AND client
#   Absent   : falls back to Vercel's own `VERCEL_URL`, then to
#              `http://localhost:3000`.
#
# Drives canonical URLs, `sitemap.ts`, Open Graph image URLs and the auth
# callback. Set it to the CANONICAL host - whichever of `cambridge-ellis.org` or
# `www.cambridge-ellis.org` the school's printed and indexed URLs use - so that
# the canonical tags, the sitemap and the host redirect all agree. A
# canonical-host flip invalidates external links as surely as changing a path.
#
# Shape: https://www.cambridge-ellis.org   (no trailing slash)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REVALIDATE_SECRET
#   Required : no
#   Exposure : server only
#   Absent   : `/api/revalidate` returns 404 - NOT 401.
#
# The 404 is deliberate: a 401 advertises that the endpoint exists. An
# unconfigured revalidation hook should be invisible, not merely closed.
#
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
REVALIDATE_SECRET=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CRON_SECRET
#   Required : no
#   Exposure : server only
#   Absent   : `/api/cleanup/orphans` returns 404, so the nightly sweep is
#              INERT rather than open. Same reasoning as above.
#
# The sweep itself is guarded independently of this secret: it only ever deletes
# expired upload reservations, orphaned quarantine objects, trashed media past
# its retention window and aged security events. It never touches a stored asset
# or a row carrying a legacy reference, which is what protects the migrated
# corpus during the window before the asset upload runs.
#
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
CRON_SECRET=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ANALYTICS_DISABLED
#   Required : no
#   Exposure : server only
#   Values   : the exact string `true` disables the tags; anything else,
#              including an empty value, leaves them enabled
#   Absent   : both analytics tags render. This is the production setting -
#              leave it unset in production.
#
# Set `true` in CI so the bulk route sweep and the Lighthouse runs never fill
# the school's real reporting with synthetic pageviews. Exactly one CI job runs
# with the tags ENABLED, and it intercepts both vendor hosts, so the assertions
# about the script nonce are real without any request leaving the runner.
#
# This flag gates whether the tags render. It does NOT hold their identifiers -
# see "DELIBERATELY ABSENT" at the end of this file.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
ANALYTICS_DISABLED=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CSP_MODE
#   Required : no - defaults to `report-only`
#   Exposure : server only
#   Values   : report-only | enforce      (a CLOSED enumeration)
#   Absent   : `report-only`, the safe end of the range.
#
# `proxy.ts` is the sole owner of the Content Security Policy and builds ONE
# policy string; this variable chooses only the header NAME it is sent under -
# `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only` or `Content-Security-Policy`. A policy
# split across two owners produces two headers, which browsers intersect, and
# that is how a working nonce policy silently becomes unsatisfiable.
#
# The enumeration is closed on purpose: a typo fails validation in `lib/env.ts`
# at import rather than silently disabling a security header. Defaulting to
# report-only means a variable missing from the Vercel project cannot produce an
# unintended enforcing policy.
#
# Promotion is a written procedure, not a judgement call: query `security_events`
# for kind `csp` over the trailing seven days, confirm zero violations from
# non-extension sources, then set `enforce`, redeploy and re-run the security
# suite against production. Report-only mode is only meaningful because the
# report collection endpoint exists to receive the violations.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
CSP_MODE=report-only


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HSTS_MAX_AGE
#   Required : no - defaults to `300`
#   Exposure : server only
#   Values   : 300 | 31536000             (a CLOSED enumeration, seconds)
#   Absent   : `300` - five minutes, the safe end of the range.
#
# `proxy.ts` emits `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=<value>` and NEVER adds
# `includeSubDomains` or `preload`, because the schema admits no value that
# carries them. Those two directives stay unavailable until a subdomain audit
# confirms that every `cambridge-ellis.org` subdomain - mail, any legacy host,
# any vendor CNAME - serves valid HTTPS AND the school signs off. Preload is
# effectively irreversible, and it would break any subdomain still on HTTP;
# widening this enumeration to admit them is itself a reviewed change.
#
# Raise to `31536000` (one year) only AFTER the production domain is attached
# and has served real traffic cleanly for a full day with no mixed-content
# reports. A long max-age on a host that then has to be rolled back is a
# self-inflicted outage: rollback is repointing DNS, and HSTS would prevent it.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
HSTS_MAX_AGE=300


# =============================================================================
# VERCEL ENVIRONMENT SCOPING
# =============================================================================
#
#   Per environment (Development / Preview / Production):
#       CONTENT_SOURCE
#       NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL
#       NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY
#       NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL
#
#   Production-scoped only:
#       REVALIDATE_SECRET
#       CRON_SECRET
#       SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY      (see the warning on that key above)
#
#   CI only:
#       ANALYTICS_DISABLED=true
#
#   `CSP_MODE` and `HSTS_MAX_AGE` are set per environment as the staged rollout
#   above reaches each stage; both are safe to omit entirely.


# =============================================================================
# LOCAL AND CI ONLY - NOT KEYS IN THIS TEMPLATE
# =============================================================================
#
# `supabase start` prints a local API URL and local anon/publishable and
# service-role keys for the containerised stack. CI writes them to
# `.env.test.local`, which is gitignored by the `.env.*` pattern and MUST NEVER
# be committed - those keys are well-known development values, and committing
# them trains everyone to ignore the secret scanner.
#
# Run both from this directory (`nextjs/`), which is where the pinned Supabase
# CLI is installed - `npm run` puts it on PATH, so no manual export is needed:
#
#   npm run db:start                    # wraps `supabase start`
#   npx supabase status -o env          # then map into ./.env.test.local:
#                                       #   API_URL         -> NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL
#                                       #   PUBLISHABLE_KEY -> NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY
#                                       #   SECRET_KEY      -> SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY
#
# The authenticated end-to-end specs FAIL rather than skip when that stack is
# absent. CI always provides it, so absence means a broken runner, not a valid
# state, and a conditional skip would let an empty run report success.
#
# Do not promote any of these into live entries in this template.


# =============================================================================
# NOT ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES AT ALL
# =============================================================================
#
# A missing item in this section produces a deployment that builds and serves
# perfectly but CANNOT BE SIGNED INTO. None of it belongs in a dotenv file.
#
# 1. THE VERCEL ROOT DIRECTORY (`nextjs`) IS A PROJECT SETTING.
#    Configured in the dashboard under Settings -> Build and Deployment -> Root
#    Directory, or through the Vercel API or CLI. It cannot be committed:
#    `.vercel/` is local link metadata holding only project and organisation
#    identifiers, and Root Directory is NOT a `vercel.json` key. Because the
#    setting lives outside the repository, verify it after the first deploy by
#    confirming in the build log that install and build ran in `nextjs/` and
#    that `nextjs/vercel.json` was applied.
#
# 2. AUTH SMTP AND EMAIL TEMPLATES - Supabase dashboard.
#    The default SMTP is rate-limited and unsuitable for a school domain.
#    Configure custom SMTP with a `no-reply@cambridge-ellis.org` sender, SPF and
#    DKIM aligned to that domain, and templates that name the school rather than
#    the vendor. This is the project's ONLY outbound mail path, and invitations
#    fail silently without it. Verify with one live send-and-accept per template.
#
# 3. THE REDIRECT ALLOWLIST - Supabase dashboard.
#    `/auth/callback`, `/auth/accept-invite` and `/auth/update-password` on the
#    production origin, plus the preview origin pattern. An address missing here
#    turns a valid invitation link into a dead end.
#
# 4. SESSION, COOKIE AND MFA SETTINGS - Supabase dashboard, mirrored in
#    `supabase/config.toml` so local behaviour matches hosted: a 1-hour JWT,
#    refresh-token rotation with reuse detection, a 7-day inactivity timeout, a
#    30-day absolute timebox, mandatory TOTP, PUBLIC SIGNUP DISABLED,
#    leaked-password protection, and a twelve-character minimum password.
#    Accounts are created by invitation only; there is no self-service signup
#    path and no policy permits one.
#
# 5. THE SEED LOAD - a one-time `psql` run over TLS.
#    `supabase/seed.sql` is the canonical database load, not a runtime input.
#    Load it with `sslmode=require`, supplying the connection string at the
#    prompt rather than storing it in a file or a shell history.
#
# 6. POSTGRESQL 17 IS A PROVISIONING REQUIREMENT.
#    The hosted project must be created on major version 17, matching the local
#    stack. Verify `select version()` before the seed load; a mismatch stops
#    cutover rather than being discovered later by a failing constraint.


# =============================================================================
# DELIBERATELY ABSENT - do not add these
# =============================================================================
#
# Each name below was considered and rejected. Adding one back would reintroduce
# the specific problem noted against it.
#
# ADMIN_EMAILS
#     An environment allowlist standing in for a role. Authorization comes from
#     the `admin_users` table plus an explicit capability matrix, checked inside
#     `security definer` database functions - so the same rules apply to a
#     direct REST call as to the application. A comma-separated list in an env
#     var cannot express a role, cannot be audited, cannot be revoked without a
#     redeploy, and would silently grant authority to any address added to it.
#
# ANALYTICS IDENTIFIERS  (no GA/GTAG/Ads/StatCounter variable of any kind)
#     The Google Ads tag and both StatCounter values are CONTENT, owned by the
#     `site_globals` table and present in the fallback JSON. That is what lets
#     them work in the keyless state and lets staff edit them afterwards
#     without a redeploy. They were hardcoded in the legacy layout template;
#     promoting them into managed data - not into environment variables - is the
#     migration. `ANALYTICS_DISABLED` above gates whether the tags render; it
#     does not carry what they report to.
#
# STATAMIC_LICENSE_KEY, STATAMIC_GIT_ENABLED, STATAMIC_GIT_AUTOMATIC,
# STATAMIC_GIT_PUSH, CP_ENABLED, CP_ROUTE, APP_KEY, APP_ENV, APP_URL, DB_*
#     Laravel and Statamic variables. No PHP executes after this migration. The
#     Git-integration flags are not merely unused: their capability moved into
#     the database as an append-only revision trail grouped by change set, plus
#     an application-owned window for replaced media bytes. The Control Panel
#     route is replaced by the fixed `/auth/*` segment and is not configurable.
#
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, RESEND_API_KEY, TAVILY_API_KEY, PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID
#     Carried by the reference implementation this project was pointed at. None
#     of those services is part of this project. Transactional email is Supabase
#     Auth SMTP, configured in the dashboard (see above); donations are handled
#     by the school's existing third-party provider, embedded rather than
#     integrated, so no payment credential is ever held here.
#
# NODE_ENV
#     Managed by the framework and the platform. Setting it by hand is how a
#     production build ends up in development mode, or a test run in production
#     mode.
